  }

  .ramp-sample {
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--color-white);
  }

  /*
   * The demonstrations run on hover or focus and never on their own. `transform` and
   * `opacity` only - the same rule every timeline on the site follows, and the reason is
   * the same here as there: anything else is a paint on the main thread.
   */
  .demo {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 90px;
    height: 22px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--color-ink-800);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .demo i {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    border-radius: inherit;
    background: var(--color-plum-500);
    transform: translateX(-100%);
    transition: transform var(--demo-duration) cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
  }

  /* No `:focus-visible` branch: nothing can focus this any more - see the markup. */
  .demo:hover i {
    transform: translateX(0);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    /*
     * The demonstrations hold their end state rather than animating into it. They are the
     * only moving thing on the page, and a visitor who has asked for less motion should
     * still be able to see what the swatch is showing.
     */
    .demo i {
      transition: none;
      transform: translateX(0);
    }
  }

  .print-cover {
    display: none;
  }

  /*
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   * The PDF - phases.md 8.10d.
   *
   * **There is no second document.** `public/brand/sonaru-brand.pdf` is this route
   * printed, by `npm run pdf`, through the Chrome Playwright already drives - the
   * precedent `npm run brand`, `npm run og` and `npm run press` all set. So anyone who
   * hits Cmd-P on `/brand` gets the file we ship, byte-for-byte the same design, and
   * there is nothing here that can drift from the page above.
   *
   * **These rules are scoped to this route and have no screen consequence**, so
   * `review-gates.md` §4 applies: `Base.astro`'s and `Header.astro`'s approved appearance
   * is untouched and their gates stay closed. `:global()` is what reaches them; Astro
   * still only ships this stylesheet on the page that declares it.
   *
   * **The four fixed layers are the whole unit.** Measured at Gate B: rendered as it
   * stands, this page is a **12.8 MB** PDF - because Chrome rasterises every
   * `position: fixed` layer *once per printed page*, and this site has four of them
   * (the ground gradient's `background-attachment: fixed`, the noise dither, the fixed
   * header, the bloom). 132 image objects, 32 of them full-page 843×1245 plates. Neutered
   * below, the same document is **775 KB**. `printBackground: false` is not the answer to
   * this: it drops the ground and leaves the site's light-on-dark type on white paper.
   * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   */
  @media print {
    /* One flat ink plate that scrolls with the document instead of sixteen fixed ones. */
    :global(html) {
      background: var(--ground-solid) !important;
      background-image: none !important;
      background-attachment: scroll !important;
    }

    /* The dither smooths a gradient that is no longer being printed. */
    :global(body::before) {
      display: none !important;
    }

    /* Site chrome is navigation. A document carries its own. */
    :global(.site-header),
    :global(.site-footer) {
      display: none !important;
    }

    /*
     * The atmosphere, and the masthead the cover replaces.
     *
     * **Anchored to `.document` rather than written bare, and that was 8.10c's own guard
     * telling us so.** `:global(.masthead)` compiles to exactly `.masthead { display: none
     * !important }`, and `utility-collisions.spec.ts` failed on it - correctly. Its premise
     * is that *"a scoped rule always carries its attribute selector, so this cannot match
     * the page's own styles"*, and a bare `:global()` is how that premise breaks: in the
     * cascade our rule is then indistinguishable from a framework utility, which is the
     * exact hazard that spec exists for. Anchoring restores the premise and says what the
     * rule means - inside this layout's article, not anywhere the class appears.
     *
     * Suppressing it in `KNOWN` was the alternative and is the wrong one: that list is for
     * names Tailwind owns, and putting ours in it spends the guard's credibility to keep a
     * selector that was too broad anyway.
     */
    :global(.document .masthead),
    :global(.document .bloom) {
      display: none !important;
    }

    /* A link offering the reader the document they are holding. */
    .download {
      display: none !important;
    }

    /*
     * `preferCSSPageSize` is on in the generator, so this is the single source of the
     * page geometry - the script passes no format and no margins. A4 rather than Letter:
     * the site writes en-GB throughout, down to its dates.
     *
     * The bottom margin is deeper than the others to clear the running footer, which
     * Chrome composes outside the page box.
     */
    @page {
      size: A4;
      margin: 16mm 16mm 20mm;
      /*
       * **The margin band is not painted by `html`, and on a dark document that shows.**
       * A root background propagates to the page *box*; the area outside it is the
       * canvas, which Chrome paints from `color-scheme` - so the first proof came out
       * `#0b0a0b` inside a `#121212` frame, with the running footer sitting in the lighter
       * band. Measured, not guessed: forcing `color-scheme: only light` turns that frame
       * **white**, which is what identifies it.
       *
       * `var()` resolves here, so this stays a token rather than becoming the one hex
       * literal outside `tokens.css`.
       */
      background: var(--ground-solid);
    }

    .print-cover {
      display: grid;
      justify-items: start;
      align-content: center;
      gap: var(--spacing-4);
      /*
       * The cover *is* the sheet, so it is the content box's own height - A4's 297mm less
       * the 16mm and 20mm `@page` margins - with its own margins zeroed. Anything short of
       * that centres the block inside a box smaller than the page and sits it visibly
       * high; anything over it spills the cover onto a second sheet.
       */
      margin: 0;
      min-height: 258mm;
      break-after: page;
    }

    .print-cover-title {
      margin: var(--spacing-4) 0 0;
      font-size: var(--text-3xl);
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: 1;
      letter-spacing: -0.02em;
      color: var(--color-white);
    }

    .print-cover-lede {
      margin: 0;
      max-width: 34em;
      font-size: var(--text-lg);
      color: var(--color-mute-200);
    }

    .print-cover-meta {
      margin: 0;
      font-size: var(--text-xs);
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--color-mute-400);
    }

    /*
     * The contents becomes a contents *page* - which is the argument that made this one
     * route instead of eight chaptered ones at 8.10c, so the printed document is where it
     * has to pay off. The box it wears on screen is a card in a scroll; on its own page
     * there is nothing for it to be a card against.
     */
    .toc {
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      border: 0;
      min-height: 258mm;
      align-content: center;
      display: grid;
      break-after: page;
    }

    .toc ol {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--spacing-3);
      font-size: var(--text-lg);
    }

    /* Every chapter opens a page. Seven chapters, seven openings. */
    h2 {
      break-before: page;
    }

    /* A heading stranded at the foot of a page is a heading for nothing. */
    h2,
    h3 {
      break-after: avoid;
    }

    /*
     * The figures are the argument, not illustrations of it - a construction diagram split
     * across a fold is a diagram that has stopped showing what it shows.
     *
     * **Only things that fit on a page are in this list, and which those are was measured
     * at the printed column width rather than guessed.** `break-inside: avoid` on a block
     * taller than the page cannot be honoured, and what Chrome does instead is push the
     * whole block to a fresh sheet and break it there anyway - so the rule buys nothing
     * and costs the remainder of the page before it. Against a content box of **986px**:
     * the contrast table is 1,816px and the misuse grid 1,028px, so both break between
     * their rows below; the type ramp is **892px and fits**, and only looked too tall when
     * it was measured at the viewport's width instead of the sheet's.
     *
     * `.rules` is here for the other failure. Each of the two rule lists is a third of a
     * page, and without this the last one split and left **a single three-line bullet
     * alone on page 22** - the document ending on an orphan.
     */
    .stage,
    .figure-block,
    .swatches,
    .ramp,
    .rules,
    .row,
    .clear-box,
    :global(.group) {
      break-inside: avoid;
    }

    /* The rows are what has to survive the fold, not the container around them. */
    tr,
    .misuse {
      break-inside: avoid;
    }

    /*
     * A table that crosses a fold and leaves its column heads on the previous sheet is a
     * page of unlabelled numbers. This is the one thing print gives you for free, and only
     * if the table is marked up with a real `<thead>` - which these two are.
     */
    thead {
      display: table-header-group;
    }

    /* Nothing on the page moves; in print there is nothing for a transition to resolve. */
    .demo i {
      transition: none !important;
      transform: translateX(0) !important;
    }
  }
</style>
